  <!-- Scientific Principles & Engineering Foundations -->
  <section class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6 shadow-sm">
    <div class="border-b border-hairline/60 pb-4">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-ink tracking-tight flex items-center gap-2">
        <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-emerald-400" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M12 6.253v13m0-13C10.832 5.477 9.246 5 7.5 5S4.168 5.477 3 6.253v13C4.168 18.477 5.754 18 7.5 18s3.332.477 4.5 1.253m0-13C13.168 5.477 14.754 5 16.5 5c1.747 0 3.332.477 4.5 1.253v13C19.832 18.477 18.247 18 16.5 18c-1.746 0-3.332.477-4.5 1.253" />
        </svg>
        {d.scienceTitle}
      </h2>
      <p class="text-xs text-muted mt-1">{d.scienceSub}</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 text-xs text-ink/80 leading-relaxed">
      <!-- 1. Dissolution & Plasticization -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-emerald-950 flex items-center justify-center text-xs border border-emerald-800">1</span>
          <h3>scCO2 Dissolution &amp; Henry's Law</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          At pressures exceeding its critical point (Pc = 73.8 bar, Tc = 31.1°C), carbon dioxide exhibits gas-like diffusivity and liquid-like density. Injected into the extruder barrel at 100–280 bar, scCO2 dissolves into the hydrated protein-starch melt following Henry's law:
        </p>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-surface-soft font-mono text-emerald-300 text-center">
          C_CO2 = K_H(T) &times; P_scCO2
        </div>
        <p>
          The dissolved gas molecules wedge between biopolymer chains, increasing free volume and acting as a molecular plasticizer. This reduces melt viscosity by 40–70% and lowers glass transition temperature (Tg), enabling smooth transport and extrusion at 45–65°C rather than standard 150°C steam melting.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 2. Explosive Micro-Cellular Nucleation -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-emerald-950 flex items-center justify-center text-xs border border-emerald-800">2</span>
          <h3>Homogeneous Micro-Cellular Nucleation</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          As the pressurized dough approaches the die orifice, it undergoes extreme thermodynamic instability characterized by an instantaneous pressure drop rate (dP/dt &gt; 2.5 GPa/s). Classical nucleation theory dictates the homogeneous bubble nucleation rate:
        </p>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-surface-soft font-mono text-emerald-300 text-center">
          J_nuc = J_0 &times; exp(&minus;&Delta;G* / k_B T)
        </div>
        <p>
          High supersaturation collapses the activation energy barrier (&Delta;G*), driving simultaneous spontaneous nucleation of billions of micro-bubbles per cm³ (10⁶–10⁸ cells/cm³). Unlike coarse steam voids (&gt;500 µm), SCFX cells average 25–45 µm with uniform closed-cell walls.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- 3. Bioactive Retention & 3-A Standards -->
      <div class="p-5 rounded-2xl bg-surface border border-hairline space-y-3">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold">
          <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-full bg-emerald-950 flex items-center justify-center text-xs border border-emerald-800">3</span>
          <h3>Non-Thermal Preservation &amp; 3-A Compliance</h3>
        </div>
        <p>
          Thermal inactivation of sensitive plant polyphenols, immunoglobulins, and probiotic spores follows Arrhenius kinetics:
        </p>
        <div class="p-2.5 rounded-lg bg-surface-soft font-mono text-emerald-300 text-center">
          k_deg = A_0 &times; exp(&minus;E_a / R T_melt)
        </div>
        <p>
          By maintaining melt temperatures strictly below 70°C, thermal degradation rate constants approach zero, retaining &gt;97% of bioactives and eliminating acrylamide (&lt;10 ppb). Product-contact surfaces strictly meet 3-A Sanitary Standard 45-03 &amp; 74-07 and EHEDG Class I with electropolished AISI 316L (Ra &le; 0.38 µm).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Comparative Technology Table -->
  <section class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 space-y-6 shadow-sm">
    <div class="border-b border-hairline/60 pb-4">
